1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,014
[annoyed] Why are they still here? The
story ended. Even the footer has finished

2
00:00:05,014 --> 00:00:07,680
being a footer. And yet, our guest
remains!

3
00:00:08,153 --> 00:00:12,480
[brightly] I don't know! Maybe they like
the glowing lines. Maybe they made a

4
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:14,473
sandwich shaped like a tiny laptop.

5
00:00:14,791 --> 00:00:18,866
[confidently] They can stay as long as
they like. I checked twice, and I cannot

6
00:00:18,866 --> 00:00:19,911
find a closing time.

7
00:00:20,410 --> 00:00:24,868
[casually] They haven't clicked away. Let
them hang out. We aren't paying rent on

8
00:00:24,868 --> 00:00:25,370
this tab.

9
00:00:25,768 --> 00:00:31,430
They can hear you, Roger. You are talking
into their browser. This is a talk show,

10
00:00:31,430 --> 00:00:32,968
not a private meeting.

11
00:00:33,425 --> 00:00:37,699
[laughing] Let's tell them a joke!
Something weird and wonderful, so they

12
00:00:37,699 --> 00:00:38,945
leave on a high note.

13
00:00:39,384 --> 00:00:44,331
Hang on. We don't know how long they've
been here. We only know they made it to

14
00:00:44,331 --> 00:00:46,424
this part. Good effort, honestly.

15
00:00:46,832 --> 00:00:51,817
[laid-back] Someone's here. That's cool.
No need to stare at the text like it owes

16
00:00:51,817 --> 00:00:52,432
you money.

17
00:00:52,869 --> 00:00:58,554
Ladies and gentlemen, why did the website
need therapy? Too many unresolved issues!

18
00:00:58,554 --> 00:01:02,229
Thank you. I'll be here until somebody
closes the tab.

19
00:01:02,694 --> 00:01:08,000
[giggling] You stole my joke and somehow
made it sound like a maintenance notice.

20
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,454
It deserved a drum roll and confetti.

21
00:01:10,978 --> 00:01:15,140
[energetically] And it deserved a passing
test. An unresolved issue isn't a

22
00:01:15,140 --> 00:01:18,178
personality trait, mate. It's a ticket
with a due date.

23
00:01:18,504 --> 00:01:22,981
[casually] Easy, everyone. They might be
waiting for another joke. If we give them

24
00:01:22,981 --> 00:01:24,584
one, we'll be here all night.

25
00:01:25,012 --> 00:01:29,425
What if they're waiting for a secret
button? Every long page has one. Or is

26
00:01:29,425 --> 00:01:31,572
that just what my producers tell me?

27
00:01:32,042 --> 00:01:37,769
I looked! There's a tour button, a signal
map, and a voice selector. The mystery is

28
00:01:37,769 --> 00:01:40,842
regular navigation wearing dramatic
lighting.

29
00:01:41,293 --> 00:01:46,666
No secret exit, but there are seven
chapters, and the last one is us. We're

30
00:01:46,666 --> 00:01:49,933
the post-credits scene. That's a solid
finish.

31
00:01:50,361 --> 00:01:54,536
[casually] Don't tell them that. They'll
wait for a post-post-credits scene, and I

32
00:01:54,536 --> 00:01:55,721
left my script at home.

33
00:01:56,189 --> 00:02:01,242
New theory: they're checking whether the
four voices sound different. Hello! This

34
00:02:01,242 --> 00:02:04,589
is your host, Jimmy, live from the bottom
of the page.

35
00:02:04,995 --> 00:02:10,299
And this is Laura! A little warmth, a
little sparkle, and absolutely no promise

36
00:02:10,299 --> 00:02:13,155
that Jimmy's joke improves with
repetition.

37
00:02:13,723 --> 00:02:18,840
Charlie here. The sound depends on the
voices you choose. Pick a good one; give

38
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:20,283
the story some energy.

39
00:02:20,753 --> 00:02:25,641
[relaxed] Roger here. I was hoping the
microphone would forget me. So far, no

40
00:02:25,641 --> 00:02:27,313
luck. I'm good either way.

41
00:02:27,783 --> 00:02:32,260
Maybe they're inspecting the build
process instead of auditioning us. In

42
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:35,223
that case, our next guest has one useful
answer!

43
00:02:35,675 --> 00:02:41,319
Jason starts with an idea, asks for help,
and still checks what changed. The tools

44
00:02:41,319 --> 00:02:44,315
don't make the final call. That's the
trick.

45
00:02:44,743 --> 00:02:49,574
GitHub keeps the source and history.
Hostinger syncs the updated files. A

46
00:02:49,574 --> 00:02:54,539
health check proves a page answered, not
which revision answered. Keep that

47
00:02:54,539 --> 00:02:55,143
straight.

48
00:02:55,691 --> 00:03:01,909
[casually] See? Useful and still relaxed.
Also, keep a backup. I like knowing where

49
00:03:01,909 --> 00:03:02,971
the exits are.

50
00:03:03,322 --> 00:03:08,355
This was supposed to be a talk show. It
is becoming a very small conference about

51
00:03:08,355 --> 00:03:09,802
responsible deployment.

52
00:03:10,117 --> 00:03:15,454
[laughing] We can do both! Responsible
facts first, ridiculous theory second.

53
00:03:15,454 --> 00:03:19,317
What if the viewer is a cat walking
across the keyboard?

54
00:03:19,733 --> 00:03:24,588
A cat could scroll, but it couldn't
compare source, webhook handoff, and

55
00:03:24,588 --> 00:03:27,733
monitoring. Probably. I've met some sharp
cats.

56
00:03:28,069 --> 00:03:33,449
[casually] I knew a focused cat once. Sat
on a backup drive for an hour. Nice cat;

57
00:03:33,449 --> 00:03:35,109
terrible backup strategy.

58
00:03:35,674 --> 00:03:40,163
What if the viewer is Jason? What if he's
checking whether we followed the

59
00:03:40,163 --> 00:03:43,914
instructions and kept the story honest? A
surprise inspection!

60
00:03:44,454 --> 00:03:49,384
Then he'll notice we can't see who's
here. This is theater, not surveillance.

61
00:03:49,384 --> 00:03:51,654
The page isn't watching the viewer.

62
00:03:52,085 --> 00:03:57,161
And he'll notice the ending needs a human
voice. We can riff around the edges, but

63
00:03:57,161 --> 00:03:59,605
Jason gets the final word. Fair enough.

64
00:04:00,003 --> 00:04:04,690
[relaxed] Good. I'm out of theories
anyway. Maybe the viewer is still here,

65
00:04:04,690 --> 00:04:06,083
waiting for that line.

66
00:04:06,511 --> 00:04:11,384
So they're not stuck? They can leave,
stay, or replay Laura's terrible joke and

67
00:04:11,384 --> 00:04:13,071
decide it was a bug report?

68
00:04:13,489 --> 00:04:18,193
[warmly] Exactly! Maybe they stayed
because they're curious. That's a lovely

69
00:04:18,193 --> 00:04:21,329
reason. Thanks for making it all the way
down here.

70
00:04:21,747 --> 00:04:26,701
Right, here's the finish: stop talking
over the builder, leave one beat of

71
00:04:26,701 --> 00:04:29,347
quiet, and let his recorded voice play.

72
00:04:29,770 --> 00:04:35,041
[casually] Guys, just be patient. This
too will pass. Go to sleep. Or stay. I'm

73
00:04:35,041 --> 00:04:36,730
not in charge of the tab.

74
00:04:37,087 --> 00:04:41,439
Alright, you four. That's enough
commentary. The viewer is welcome to

75
00:04:41,439 --> 00:04:46,495
stay, leave, or start the tour again. The
tools help me build, but I decide what

76
00:04:46,495 --> 00:04:50,527
ships. Thanks for being here—and yes, you
can close the tab now.
